2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ulrich Salchow was one of the most successful figure skaters of all-time and dominated figure skating in the early 1900s. His name was given to the figure skating jump “Salchow” that he performed for the first time in 1909'''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a specialist of the now defunct compulsories, which accounted for a large percentage of the total marks.
2 CONT
2 CONT Salchow won 10 World Championships, 1901-05 and 1907-11. He did not compete in the 1906 World Championships that were held in Munich, as he feared that he would not be judged fairly against Gilbert Fuchs of Germany. His 10 titles 
2 CONC are still a record, which he shares with Sonja Henie and Irina Rodnina. When figure skating was first contested at the Summer Olympics in London in 1908, Salchow won the title with ease. In addition, Salchow won the European Champ
2 CONC ionships a record nine times (1898-1900, 1904, 1906-07, 1909-10, 1913), placed second in the World Championships three times (1897, 1899-1900) and once at the European Championships (1901). In his early career he was Swedish natio
2 CONC nal champion in 1895-97. In 1976, he was inducted into the Figure Skating World Hall of Fame.
2 CONT
2 CONT Salchow could not defend his Olympic gold medal at Stockholm in 1912 because organizers opted out of holding skating contests. When he made one last attempt at the Olympics in 1920, it ended in a fall, ironically while attempting 
2 CONC his own jump, the Salchow. Despite this, the then 42-year-old managed to place fourth. In 1906, he published a handbook of skating, which was translated into several languages. He was also active in other sports, including cycling
2 CONC  and bobsledding.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his active career Salchow was President of the International Skating Union from 1925-37 and Chairman of the AIK in Stockholm from 1928-39. He was also chairman of the Swedish Cycling Association (1904-07), Swedish Skating As
2 CONC sociation (1917-20, 1923-32, 1935-38), and helped to found the Swedish Boxing Federation, where he was chairman from 1919-32, and he was a member of the board of the National Sports Confederation (1911-28).
2 CONT
2 CONT Salchow was an active yachtsman and worked for the Swedish newspaper Dagens Nyheter and Associated Press as a journalist. He was on the board of the Swedish Radio and a successful merchant and radio pioneer.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ''in''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.olympedia.org/athletes/97097
2 CONT
2 CONT *
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ulrich Salchow''' was a Danish-born Swedish figure skater, who dominated the sport in the first decade of the 20th century. He won the World Figure Skating Championships ten times, from 1901 to 1905 and from 1907 to 1911. This 
2 CONC is still a record, which he shares with Sonja Henie and Irina Rodnina. When figure skating was first contested at the Summer Olympic Games in London (1908), Salchow won the title with ease and became one of the oldest figure skati
2 CONC ng Olympic champions. In addition, Salchow won the European Championships a record nine times (1898–1900, 1904, 1906–1907, 1909–1910, 1913) and placed second in the World Championships three times.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1909, Salchow first landed a jump in competition in which he took off on the back inside edge and landed on the back outside edge of his other foot. This jump is now known as the Salchow jump in his honor.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his competitive days, Salchow remained active in the sport and was president of the International Skating Union (ISU) from 1925 to 1937. Furthermore, he was the chairman of AIK in Stockholm between 1928 and 1939 – the leadin
2 CONC g Swedish club in football, ice hockey, bandy, tennis and other sports. ''(Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 4.0)''
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulrich_Salchow
