1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Earlier Settlers of Hong Kong, Not Opium Smugglers
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Nicolia Duus'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicolia, alias Nicholas, Duus (b. 1807, Hals, Denmark – d. December 5, 1861, Hong Kong) was a mariner and merchant who came to Hong Kong in 1837 after having spent five years in Calcutta, presumably working also as a merchant. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Duus ran a trading firm and store, N. Duus & Co., at 18 Queen's Road retailing foods, wines, perfumes and hardware. Friend of China in its October 5, 1843 issue had named N. Duus as one of the three big grocers in Hong Kong: F.H. 
2 CONC Tiedeman of an unnumbered godown and store in Queen’s Road, N Duus at 18 Queen’s Road, and Pain & Co. at 2 Magistracy Street. I found an advertisement he placed in Friend of China on September 7, 1843 that said, “For sale French c
2 CONC ognac and English brandy in hogsheads. Manila rum and Java arrack in cases. Apply N. Duus, 18 Queen’s Road.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT He bought and sold lorchas and even supplied patent toilets. The August 5, 1845 issue of Friend of China described the popular item as “a patent water closet for an upper story”. The firm was the Hong Kong agent for London book pu
2 CONC blisher - Ingram, Cooke & Co. According to Friend of China, N. Duus started supplying water to ships at their anchorages with the use of a waterboat fitted with tanks and a force pump as early as March 1844. This would put N. Duus
2 CONC  the first company to provide this service in Hong Kong, two years ahead of Bowra, Humphreys & Co.
2 CONT
2 CONT more ...http://hongkongsfirst.blogspot.hk/2011/11/early-settlers-not-opium-smugglers_25.html
